ion-capture/14083592/assets/c8e5605672eaf10428132773817bccd6795c20281686d5bcb272370619b4b81d.png) as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/6f956b080575cea02674247877e555d3158f184f55bff45147011be97c4b474b.png)" 13194139536191 true] [17592186119583 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/fea24c391122278ffe20ec0f6a7d704b835aca18c3bdcd7395aeeea0f593a2bf.png)" 13194139536191 false] [17592186119583 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/4b3aa22ba1342c45d422c227258837ae080ee705f2580437c775bd2beaf8afeb.png)" 13194139536191 true] [17592186119583 1145 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/368f42200b9366547bdff9840a894b63d3df43722052ecb1a4e15e2306b4e56a.png)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$" 13194139536191 false] [17592186119583 1145 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/cafe7ac2b6224a50a288cabf6b6838c6a85a722404c9ebd20238f689f196b841.png)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$" 13194139536191 true] [17592186119750 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388092 13194139536191 true] [17592186119750 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388120 13194139536191 true] [17592186119750 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388127 13194139536191 true] [17592186119751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388099 13194139536191 true] [17592186119751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388134 13194139536191 true] [17592186119751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388141 13194139536191 true] [17592186119752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388113 13194139536191 true] [17592186119766 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388106 13194139536191 true] [17592186120176 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/1f9599192c1979f40fc519458019ed3296d9a5ef9048aaed41aab973c62e0be8.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." 13194139536191 false] [17592186120176 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/1f9599192c1979f40fc519458019ed3296d9a5ef9048aaed41aab973c62e0be8.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." 13194139536191 true] [17592186343557 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/assets/1647eb5cca32c6fdc99e23507db151a8299b29bfa70ebfbaa941e17fe4638586.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." 13194139536191 false] [17592186343557 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/1647eb5cca32c6fdc99e23507db151a8299b29